== mini-troff -T md sample.1: a manual page converted, its first lines
\title{SAMPLE}{1}

# NAME
sample, example − a page to try the macros of man(6) on

# SYNOPSIS
`sample` \[ `‑cds` ] \[ `‑o` *file* ] \[ *name ...* ]

# DESCRIPTION
*Sample* does nothing: it is a page written to be typeset.  Its first
paragraph is long enough to be put on several lines, so that one sees
where a line is ended when the next word does not fit.  A sentence ends
here.  Not after e.g.⁠ this one, nor after `a.out` in the font of what
is typed; it does after `one`.  A word with a hyphen may be cut after
it, as in a twenty-seven-letter right-to-left never-ending word; not an
option's, as
`‑long‑option‑name‑that‑is‑not‑to‑be‑cut‑anywhere‑in‑its‑letters`, and
not a minus: `−5`.

The options are:

`‑c`
:    Complement the set.  A tag shorter than its width has its text on its
     line.

`‑d`
:    Delete.

`‑o` *file*
:    The tag is wider than five letters: its text starts below.
